		<section name="node">
			<attribute key="id" type="int">46</attribute>
			<attribute key="label" type="String">Following are some hints.
1. You can strike off on a paper. But how do we do it in a program?
2. What did we do in the above logic? We are simply tallying the characters between the 2 strings.
3. So, it is sufficient to tally the characters.
3.1 Take a character from the 1st string.
3.2 Count how many times it appears in the 1st string. Write it down.
3.3 Repeat this for all the characters in the 1st string.
3.4 Take a character from the 2nd string.
3.5 Count how many times it appears in the 2nd string.
3.6 Check if this count matches with the count in the 1st string.
3.7 If yes, continue and repeat for the next character from the 2nd string.
3.8 If no, then the 2 strings are not anagrams.
3.9 By the time we reach the end of the 2nd string, if there are no mismatches, then the 2 strings are anagrams.

These hints are almost like an algorithm, but one major point is not mentioned in these hints.

How do we store the counts of every character of the 1st string?
This is where our data structure skills come into picture.</attribute>
			<section name="graphics">
				<attribute key="x" type="double">4959.386694089368</attribute>
				<attribute key="y" type="double">2839.3641032865025</attribute>
				<attribute key="w" type="double">474.45073858356045</attribute>
				<attribute key="h" type="double">324.99736243119185</attribute>
				<attribute key="type" type="String">rectangle</attribute>
				<attribute key="raisedBorder" type="boolean">false</attribute>
				<attribute key="fill" type="String">#FFCC00</attribute>
				<attribute key="outline" type="String">#000000</attribute>
			</section>
			<section name="LabelGraphics">
				<attribute key="text" type="String">Following are some hints.
1. You can strike off on a paper. But how do we do it in a program?
2. What did we do in the above logic? We are simply tallying the characters between the 2 strings.
3. So, it is sufficient to tally the characters.
3.1 Take a character from the 1st string.
3.2 Count how many times it appears in the 1st string. Write it down.
3.3 Repeat this for all the characters in the 1st string.
3.4 Take a character from the 2nd string.
3.5 Count how many times it appears in the 2nd string.
3.6 Check if this count matches with the count in the 1st string.
3.7 If yes, continue and repeat for the next character from the 2nd string.
3.8 If no, then the 2 strings are not anagrams.
3.9 By the time we reach the end of the 2nd string, if there are no mismatches, then the 2 strings are anagrams.

These hints are almost like an algorithm, but one major point is not mentioned in these hints.

How do we store the counts of every character of the 1st string?
This is where our data structure skills come into picture.</attribute>
				<attribute key="fontSize" type="int">12</attribute>
				<attribute key="fontName" type="String">Dialog</attribute>
				<attribute key="configuration" type="String">CroppingLabel</attribute>
				<attribute key="contentWidth" type="double">474.45073858356045</attribute>
				<attribute key="contentHeight" type="double">322.96875</attribute>
				<attribute key="autoSizePolicy" type="String">node_width</attribute>
				<attribute key="anchor" type="String">c</attribute>
			</section>
		</section>
		<section name="node">
			<attribute key="id" type="int">47</attribute>
			<attribute key="label" type="String">There are multiple ways to store the counts of characters present in a string.

1. Variables approach: Use variables like a_count, b_count, c_count etc. to store the counts.
2. Arrays approach: Use 2 arrays. In one array store the character, and in the second array store the corresponding count.
3. Hashmap approach: Use a hashmap to store &lt;character, count> key value pairs.</attribute>
			<section name="graphics">
				<attribute key="x" type="double">4986.290258197595</attribute>
				<attribute key="y" type="double">3134.5165312163676</attribute>
				<attribute key="w" type="double">396.4802232032313</attribute>
				<attribute key="h" type="double">155.12383464378092</attribute>
				<attribute key="type" type="String">rectangle</attribute>
				<attribute key="raisedBorder" type="boolean">false</attribute>
				<attribute key="fill" type="String">#FFCC00</attribute>
				<attribute key="outline" type="String">#000000</attribute>
			</section>
			<section name="LabelGraphics">
				<attribute key="text" type="String">There are multiple ways to store the counts of characters present in a string.

1. Variables approach: Use variables like a_count, b_count, c_count etc. to store the counts.
2. Arrays approach: Use 2 arrays. In one array store the character, and in the second array store the corresponding count.
3. Hashmap approach: Use a hashmap to store &lt;character, count> key value pairs.</attribute>
